cmd_relocations() {
  local disks dest="${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/Colima/_disks"; disks="$(colima_disks)"
  cat <<EOS
=== Native-UI relocations (the script never auto-moves these) ===
Some apps cache the absolute path to their data internally. A
symlink works most of the time, but the native UIs handle the
transition cleanly including index rebuilds and metadata fixups.
Use these for: Docker Desktop, Colima, OrbStack, Photos, Music, iMovie,
Final Cut.

Suggested destinations (HOT drive: ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<not configured>}):

Docker Desktop disk image (typically the biggest single win)
  1. Quit any running containers.
  2. Docker Desktop > Settings > Resources > Advanced > Disk image location.
  3. Change to ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/Docker/
  4. Apply & Restart. Docker copies the image to the new location
     and keeps the old until you confirm.
  5. After it boots cleanly: 'docker ps' should work and
     'docker run --rm hello-world' should succeed.
  6. The original ~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker can then be
     deleted from Docker's UI prompt (or manually after verification).

Colima VM disks (Colima has no setting for this; a symlink does it)
EOS
  if [[ "$(physical_path "$disks")" == "$(physical_path "$dest")" ]]; then
    cat <<EOS
  Already done: "$disks" resolves to "$dest".
  That folder holds the live VM disks. Keep it.
EOS
  else
    cat <<EOS
  1. Stop every Colima VM: 'colima list' names them, then
     'colima stop --profile NAME' for each.
  2. mkdir -p "${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/Colima"
     cp -aH "$disks" "${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/Colima/"
     cp keeps the disks sparse on an APFS drive; rsync writes each one
     out at full size. -H copies the disks, not the link, when _disks
     already links to another drive. If cp fails, stop here.
  3. mv "$disks" "$disks.old"
     ln -s "$dest" "$disks"
  4. Start each VM: 'docker ps' should list your containers and
     'docker run --rm hello-world' should succeed.
  5. Then delete "$disks.old".
     If it was a link to a folder other than "$dest",
     that folder holds the old disks: delete it too.
EOS
  fi
  cat <<EOS

OrbStack data
  1. OrbStack > Settings > Storage > Data location.
  2. Change to ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/OrbStack/
  3. OrbStack starts empty there: it does not copy your containers,
     images or volumes. The old disk stays in
     ~/Library/Group Containers/HUAQ24HBR6.dev.orbstack/data
     until you delete it.
  4. Quit OrbStack before you unplug the drive. OrbStack 2.2.3 replaced
     an external disk with an empty one after an unplug
     (orbstack/orbstack#2723).

Photos Library
  1. Quit Photos.
  2. Move ~/Pictures/Photos\ Library.photoslibrary to ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/
  3. Hold Option (⌥) while launching Photos.
  4. Click "Other Library..." and pick the moved bundle.
  5. Photos > Settings > General > Use as System Photo Library.

Music.app Library
  1. Quit Music.
  2. Music > Settings > Files > Music Media folder location > Change…
  3. Point at ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/Music\ Media (creates folder).
  4. File > Library > Organize Library… > Consolidate files.
  5. Move ~/Music/Music/Music\ Library.musiclibrary to the new location
     via File > Library > Choose Library… if you also want metadata external.

iMovie / Final Cut Pro Library
  1. Quit iMovie / Final Cut.
  2. Move ~/Movies/iMovie\ Library.imovielibrary to ${OFFLOAD_HOT:-<HOT>}/
  3. Double-click the moved .imovielibrary bundle to relaunch from new location.
  4. (Final Cut: File > Open Library… > select the moved .fcpbundle.)
EOS
}

